      <ArticleTitle>Single-stage versus double-stage laryngotracheal reconstruction for grade III subglottic stenosis in children: a multicenter cohort.</ArticleTitle>
      <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2026.161999</ELocationID>
      <Abstract>
        <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The best strategy for <i>grade III</i> subglottic stenosis is debated.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective cohort of 84 children (2015-2024) across 3 centers.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Decannulation was achieved in 88% after single-stage and 91% after double-stage reconstruction (p=0.62).</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Single-stage reconstruction had similar decannulation with shorter stay.</AbstractText>
      </Abstract>
